    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If multiple reliability metrics (split-half, test-retest, year-over-year) are tested without predefined primary endpoint, false positive risk increases."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.35,
    "untestable": 0.35,
    "artifact": 0.10,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The intraclass correlation (ICC) of the team-specific defensive effect on opponents' second-half minus first-half scoring, adjusted for halftime score margin, opponent offensive strength, location, and rest, estimated from a hierarchical model over regular-season games from 2022-23 through 2025-26.",
    "unit": "Team-season (defense); 30 teams × 4 complete regular seasons = up to 120 units, only those with a minimum of 60 games retained.",
    "sample": "All completed regular-season games from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and the completed portion of 2025-26 (games through 2026-04-13). Playoffs excluded to avoid structural differences. The 2025-26 partial season will be censored at 82 games or the end of the regular season; only games with full data will be used.",
    "power": "Simulation-based: with 120 groups and ~80 observations per group, assuming an ICC of 0.10 and residual variance typical of per-game opponent margin changes, the 90% credible interval width for the ICC is expected to be approximately ±0.08. This provides adequate power to distinguish ICC > 0.15 from zero but limited power to precisely estimate very small values.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects: halftime score difference (cubic spline), opponent's season offensive rating (as a proxy for their typical second-half performance), home/away indicator, days of rest for both the offense and defense, and a categorical variable for quarter of season (1–4) to capture tactical evolution. The model also includes random intercepts for opponent-offense-season to account for variation in opponent quality beyond the overall rating.",
    "falsification": "1) Apply the same model with the outcome being first-half opponent points (per-possession), where the halftime adjustment component is absent; if the trait is purely a halftime-adjustment effect, the between-team variance should be near zero. 2) Run a leave-one-season-out predictive check: fit the model to three seasons and compute the correlation between predicted and observed team effects in the held-out season. 3) Replace the outcome with a permuted label (team-defense scrambled) to confirm that the estimated ICC collapses to zero.",
    "multiplicity": "The primary endpoint is the posterior median ICC (team-season level) from the fully adjusted model, tested against a threshold of 0.10 (negligible) with a pre-registered decision rule. Secondary metrics (year-over-year stability, split-half r) are reported only with multiplicity-adjusted intervals (Bonferroni) if compared to thresholds.",
    "decision_rule": "Declare the trait 'supported as reliable' if the 90% highest posterior density interval for the team-season ICC lies entirely above 0.15, AND the leave-one-season-out predictive correlation is significantly positive (p < 0.01) and at least 0.20. If the ICC interval is below 0.10 and the predictive correlation is non-significant, reject as unreliable. Otherwise, result is inconclusive (untestable).",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API per-game data as of 2026-10-06, covering 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and the completed portion of 2025-26 regular season (all games through 2026-04-13)."
  }
}

